Echo the remote's EFS option only when the remote sent one (CVE-2022-42895).

Applied by kernel/build-bt-modules.sh to its own copy of the tree, never by build-image.sh:
the L2CAP core is in bluetooth.ko, a module, so this changes no byte of the image.

Why: l2cap_parse_conf_req() declares `struct l2cap_conf_efs efs;` uninitialised and fills it only
when the configuration request carries an EFS option (remote_efs = 1). In ERTM mode with
FLAG_EFS_ENABLE set, the vanilla 4.14.52 code copies efs into the channel and sends it back to the
remote in the configuration response whether or not it was filled, leaking kernel stack bytes to
an unauthenticated peer in radio range. Reachable only with FLAG_EFS_ENABLE, i.e. with High Speed
enabled from userspace (mgmt), which this build does not do; fixed anyway because the fix is one
condition.

Source: mainline commit b1a2cd50c035 "Bluetooth: L2CAP: Fix attempting to access uninitialized
memory" (v6.1). Its change is the `remote_efs &&` condition on that block.

Changes: the ERTM branch of l2cap_parse_conf_req() uses and echoes efs only if remote_efs is set.

--- a/net/bluetooth/l2cap_core.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/l2cap_core.c
@@ -3485,7 +3485,8 @@
 			l2cap_add_conf_opt(&ptr, L2CAP_CONF_RFC,
 					   sizeof(rfc), (unsigned long) &rfc, endptr - ptr);
 
-			if (test_bit(FLAG_EFS_ENABLE, &chan->flags)) {
+			if (remote_efs &&
+			    test_bit(FLAG_EFS_ENABLE, &chan->flags)) {
 				chan->remote_id = efs.id;
 				chan->remote_stype = efs.stype;
 				chan->remote_msdu = le16_to_cpu(efs.msdu);
